{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.737b261c90","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-22","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many conservationists favor protecting large, continuous areas of habitat, arguing that big reserves support more species and allow animals to roam, find mates, and adapt to changing conditions.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large reserves have clear advantages, but a network of many smaller protected areas can sometimes preserve more total species. Because small reserves can be spread across different habitats, such as wetlands, grasslands, and forests, they may capture a wider range of plants and animals than a single large reserve located in one type of landscape.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that small reserves are always better than large reserves","By rejecting the idea that animals need to roam or find mates","By acknowledging the advantages of large reserves while arguing that several smaller reserves may sometimes protect more species","By arguing that reserves should be located only in forests"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 grants \"clear advantages\" but proposes that networks of small reserves can capture more habitats and species. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3e7104d62a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-22","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many conservationists favor protecting large, continuous areas of habitat, arguing that big reserves support more species and allow animals to roam, find mates, and adapt to changing conditions.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large reserves have clear advantages, but a network of many smaller protected areas can sometimes preserve more total species. Because small reserves can be spread across different habitats, such as wetlands, grasslands, and forests, they may capture a wider range of plants and animals than a single large reserve located in one type of landscape.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree that","choices":["wetlands contain fewer species than forests do.","protected areas play an important role in conserving species.","small reserves are more expensive to manage than large reserves.","animals should not be allowed to move between reserves."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Both discuss how reserves can best preserve species; they differ on size. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e0e8f690c5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-23","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Traditionally, historians have credited a single inventor with creating the first practical telephone, pointing to the patent he filed and the famous first call he placed to his assistant.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Patent records and laboratory notebooks show that several inventors in different countries were developing similar voice-transmitting devices during the same years, often building on one another's published work. Crediting one person, some historians now argue, obscures the collaborative and competitive process through which the telephone actually emerged.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that one inventor deserves all the credit for the telephone","By arguing that crediting a single inventor overlooks the work of many people who developed similar devices at the same time","By claiming that the famous first call never took place","By suggesting that the telephone was invented much later than historians believe"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 points to several inventors working at once and says single credit \"obscures\" that process. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ba3ae84692","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-23","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Traditionally, historians have credited a single inventor with creating the first practical telephone, pointing to the patent he filed and the famous first call he placed to his assistant.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Patent records and laboratory notebooks show that several inventors in different countries were developing similar voice-transmitting devices during the same years, often building on one another's published work. Crediting one person, some historians now argue, obscures the collaborative and competitive process through which the telephone actually emerged.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?","choices":["Text 2 challenges the traditional account described in Text 1.","Text 2 provides additional details that support the account in Text 1.","Text 2 describes the invention of a different device.","Text 2 summarizes Text 1 without offering a new perspective."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 presents evidence against crediting one person, the view in Text 1. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4755849407","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-24","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating late at night inevitably leads to weight gain, because the body supposedly burns fewer calories while sleeping.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Controlled studies that give participants the same total calories at different times of day have generally found little difference in weight change. Late-night eating may be linked to weight gain in surveys, but that link often reflects what people eat at night, such as high-calorie snacks, and how much they eat overall, not the timing itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that surveys about eating habits are always accurate","By agreeing that the body burns no calories during sleep","By arguing that the total amount and type of food people eat matter more than the time at which they eat it","By claiming that eating at night causes weight loss"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 cites studies showing timing has little effect when calories are equal and points to what and how much people eat. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.dd17a2e8c9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-25","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Critics sometimes dismiss graphic novels as simplified versions of \"real\" literature, suitable mainly for readers who struggle with traditional books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Reading a graphic novel well requires skills that prose alone does not develop. Readers must interpret how images and words interact, notice details in the artwork that contradict or complicate the text, and infer what happens in the gaps between panels. Far from being a simplified form, the graphic novel asks readers to combine two ways of making meaning at once.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that prose novels contain no images","By arguing that graphic novels demand complex interpretive skills rather than offering a simplified reading experience","By suggesting that graphic novels should replace traditional literature in schools","By agreeing that graphic novels are mainly for struggling readers"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 describes the skills graphic novels require and says they are \"far from being a simplified form.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.37735822b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-26","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of four-day school weeks argue that the schedule saves districts money on transportation and utilities and gives teachers more time to plan.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In several rural districts that adopted four-day weeks, savings turned out to be smaller than expected, typically a few percent of the budget. Some families also reported difficulty finding child care on the fifth day. Although teachers generally liked the schedule, results for student achievement were mixed across districts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By noting that the savings may be modest and that the schedule can create other challenges","By claiming that four-day weeks increase transportation costs","By arguing that teachers dislike four-day weeks","By agreeing that four-day weeks clearly improve student achievement"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 reports small savings, child-care problems, and mixed achievement results. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e879c5905c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-27","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many birdwatchers believe that feeding wild birds in winter helps them survive the cold months by providing extra energy when natural food is scarce.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Winter feeding can help some birds survive, but it can also have unintended effects. In one region, feeders encouraged certain species to stay farther north instead of migrating, and crowds at feeders helped spread diseases among birds. Researchers suggest keeping feeders clean and spacing them apart to reduce these risks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?","choices":["It qualifies the view in Text 1 by describing possible drawbacks of the practice.","It describes a different practice unrelated to Text 1.","It provides evidence that the view in Text 1 is entirely mistaken.","It restates the view in Text 1 in more technical language."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 accepts the benefit but adds risks and precautions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.499b0e8284","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-28","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>When a coastal city considered building a seawall to protect against rising seas, many residents supported the plan, arguing that a solid wall was the most reliable defense against flooding.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Seawalls can protect the land directly behind them, but they often increase erosion on nearby beaches by reflecting wave energy. Some coastal engineers recommend combining smaller structures with restored dunes and marshes, which absorb wave energy and can adjust as sea levels change.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that seawalls provide no protection at all","By agreeing that a solid wall is the only reliable defense","By arguing that the city faces no risk from rising seas","By pointing out that seawalls can cause problems nearby and that natural features may offer a more adaptable form of protection"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 notes erosion caused by seawalls and recommends dunes and marshes that \"can adjust.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.34a575263e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-28","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>When a coastal city considered building a seawall to protect against rising seas, many residents supported the plan, arguing that a solid wall was the most reliable defense against flooding.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Seawalls can protect the land directly behind them, but they often increase erosion on nearby beaches by reflecting wave energy. Some coastal engineers recommend combining smaller structures with restored dunes and marshes, which absorb wave energy and can adjust as sea levels change.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a restored marsh would most likely be preferred over a seawall because it","choices":["reflects wave energy toward nearby beaches.","can absorb wave energy and adapt to changing sea levels.","prevents all flooding in the city.","is cheaper to build than any other structure."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Marshes \"absorb wave energy and can adjust as sea levels change.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.32dd339689","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data8-176","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating fat makes people gain weight more than eating any other nutrient.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fat contains more calories per gram than protein or carbohydrates, but studies comparing low-fat and low-carbohydrate diets with equal calories have found similar weight loss on average. What matters most for weight appears to be total calories and whether people can stick with a diet over time.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that when calories are equal, low-fat diets do not produce more weight loss, and that total calories and adherence matter most","By agreeing that fat causes more weight gain than any other nutrient regardless of calories","By arguing that diets should contain no carbohydrates","By claiming that fat contains fewer calories than protein"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Equal-calorie diets produced similar results. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e1b3abf493","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data8-176","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating fat makes people gain weight more than eating any other nutrient.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fat contains more calories per gram than protein or carbohydrates, but studies comparing low-fat and low-carbohydrate diets with equal calories have found similar weight loss on average. What matters most for weight appears to be total calories and whether people can stick with a diet over time.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that fat","choices":["has no calories.","should be eliminated from diets.","contains more calories per gram than protein or carbohydrates.","is the only cause of weight gain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Fat contains more calories per gram.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8335d1f658","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data8-176","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating fat makes people gain weight more than eating any other nutrient.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fat contains more calories per gram than protein or carbohydrates, but studies comparing low-fat and low-carbohydrate diets with equal calories have found similar weight loss on average. What matters most for weight appears to be total calories and whether people can stick with a diet over time.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, an important factor for weight loss is","choices":["whether people can stick with a diet over time.","the color of foods.","whether a diet is low in fat specifically.","the time of day meals are eaten."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Whether people can stick with a diet over time.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.fe1b747c30","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-303","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many city leaders argue that free public transit would dramatically reduce car traffic. If riders no longer had to pay, they reason, far more commuters would leave their cars at home.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When one mid-sized city eliminated bus fares, ridership rose by about a third. Traffic surveys, however, showed little change in car trips. Most new riders had previously walked or biked; few had switched from driving. Researchers concluded that for many drivers, travel time and convenience mattered more than cost.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that free transit greatly reduces car traffic","By arguing that making transit free may not convince many drivers to switch, because cost is not their main consideration","By claiming that free transit decreases bus ridership","By suggesting that cities should raise transit fares"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 found ridership rose but car trips barely changed, since drivers cared more about time and convenience. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7b6683bae5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-303","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many city leaders argue that free public transit would dramatically reduce car traffic. If riders no longer had to pay, they reason, far more commuters would leave their cars at home.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When one mid-sized city eliminated bus fares, ridership rose by about a third. Traffic surveys, however, showed little change in car trips. Most new riders had previously walked or biked; few had switched from driving. Researchers concluded that for many drivers, travel time and convenience mattered more than cost.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, most of the new bus riders were people who","choices":["stopped using transit after a year.","had previously driven cars.","had previously walked or biked.","had never traveled in the city."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Most new riders had previously walked or biked.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d4fef6f431","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-303","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many city leaders argue that free public transit would dramatically reduce car traffic. If riders no longer had to pay, they reason, far more commuters would leave their cars at home.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When one mid-sized city eliminated bus fares, ridership rose by about a third. Traffic surveys, however, showed little change in car trips. Most new riders had previously walked or biked; few had switched from driving. Researchers concluded that for many drivers, travel time and convenience mattered more than cost.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?","choices":["Text 2 presents evidence that complicates the prediction made in Text 1.","Text 2 restates Text 1's argument without adding evidence.","Text 2 describes an unrelated transportation policy.","Text 2 provides evidence that fully confirms Text 1."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The prediction of reduced traffic was not borne out. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.db48bcca26","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-304","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutritionists recommend that people eat many small meals throughout the day instead of three larger ones, arguing that frequent eating keeps metabolism running faster and helps with weight control.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Metabolism does rise slightly after every meal as the body digests food, but the total increase depends on how much is eaten, not how many times. In controlled studies where participants ate the same total calories in either three meals or six smaller ones, their total energy use and weight change were essentially the same.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that meal frequency does not change total energy use when total calories are the same","By claiming that eating fewer meals causes weight gain","By agreeing that six small meals speed up metabolism more than three meals","By suggesting that digestion does not affect metabolism"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 says energy use depends on total food, and studies found no difference. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.24404ac8ce","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-305","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Historians have often portrayed a certain nineteenth-century inventor as a lone genius who developed the electric light bulb through sheer persistence, testing thousands of materials by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Laboratory records show that the inventor worked with a large team of machinists, chemists, and assistants who carried out many of the experiments. Moreover, several earlier inventors had already produced working bulbs, though none lasted long. The inventor's achievement, some historians now argue, was organizing collaborative research and improving existing designs, not working alone.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions \"several earlier inventors\" mainly to","choices":["explain why the inventor hired assistants.","show that the basic idea of the bulb did not originate with this inventor alone.","argue that earlier bulbs were superior.","prove that the inventor stole their designs."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Earlier working bulbs challenge the lone-genius story. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b11bebeeba","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-306","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Supporters of homework-free policies in elementary schools argue that homework adds stress without improving learning, and that children benefit more from play and family time after school.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on elementary homework shows mixed results. Short, well-designed assignments, such as reading with a parent for fifteen minutes, have been linked to gains in reading. Long worksheets that repeat class material show little benefit and can cause frustration. The question, according to many researchers, is not whether to assign homework but what kind to assign.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which kind of homework is most likely to benefit elementary students?","choices":["Brief reading time with a parent","Long worksheets repeating class material","No assignments of any kind","Homework assigned only on weekends"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Reading with a parent \"has been linked to gains in reading.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5654d4fef3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-308","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiobooks are a lesser form of reading, claiming that listeners absorb less of a book's meaning than people who read print.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one study, participants who listened to a short story and participants who read it scored similarly on questions about plot and character. Listeners actually recalled more details about characters' emotions, which the researchers attributed to narrators' vocal expression. The researchers noted, however, that readers found it easier to reread difficult passages.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that audiobooks should replace printed books","By suggesting that narrators make stories harder to follow","By arguing that evidence shows listeners understand stories about as well as readers do, though each format has some advantages","By agreeing that listeners understand far less than readers"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 reports similar comprehension, with advantages for each format. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1f4bc692ab","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-308","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiobooks are a lesser form of reading, claiming that listeners absorb less of a book's meaning than people who read print.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one study, participants who listened to a short story and participants who read it scored similarly on questions about plot and character. Listeners actually recalled more details about characters' emotions, which the researchers attributed to narrators' vocal expression. The researchers noted, however, that readers found it easier to reread difficult passages.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions that readers found it easier to reread difficult passages mainly to","choices":["explain why the story was chosen.","criticize the study's design.","acknowledge an advantage of print reading.","show that audiobooks are superior in every way."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It presents a balancing point in favor of print. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e738ab16bc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-309","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many parents believe that playing classical music for infants makes them smarter, an idea popularized decades ago by reports of a so-called music effect.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The original study behind the claim tested college students, not infants, and found only a small, temporary improvement on one spatial reasoning task after listening to a sonata. Later attempts to repeat the result often failed. No research has shown that simply playing classical music for babies raises their intelligence.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions that later attempts often failed mainly to","choices":["further undermine confidence in the original result.","suggest that infants dislike classical music.","show that the original study was fraudulent.","argue that more sonatas should be played."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Failed replications weaken the claim. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7339fa98d7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-310","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of standing desks claim that working while standing burns far more calories than sitting and can help office workers lose weight.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Standing does burn slightly more energy than sitting, but measurements suggest the difference is modest, roughly the equivalent of a small snack over an entire workday. Standing desks may reduce back discomfort for some workers, but they are unlikely on their own to produce significant weight loss.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that sitting burns more calories than standing","By acknowledging that standing burns somewhat more calories while arguing that the difference is too small to cause much weight loss","By arguing that standing desks always cause back pain","By agreeing that standing desks lead to major weight loss"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 grants a small increase but disputes significant weight loss. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4b17d91072","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-310","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of standing desks claim that working while standing burns far more calories than sitting and can help office workers lose weight.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Standing does burn slightly more energy than sitting, but measurements suggest the difference is modest, roughly the equivalent of a small snack over an entire workday. Standing desks may reduce back discomfort for some workers, but they are unlikely on their own to produce significant weight loss.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a possible benefit of standing desks is that they","choices":["may reduce back discomfort for some workers.","replace the need for exercise.","make workers more productive in every case.","cause substantial weight loss."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"May reduce back discomfort for some workers.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1899dd0488","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-310","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of standing desks claim that working while standing burns far more calories than sitting and can help office workers lose weight.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Standing does burn slightly more energy than sitting, but measurements suggest the difference is modest, roughly the equivalent of a small snack over an entire workday. Standing desks may reduce back discomfort for some workers, but they are unlikely on their own to produce significant weight loss.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes a difference in how the two texts view standing desks?","choices":["Text 1 opposes standing desks, while Text 2 supports them.","Text 1 focuses on back pain, while Text 2 focuses on productivity.","Text 1 relies on measurements, while Text 2 relies on opinions.","Text 1 emphasizes calorie burning, while Text 2 suggests that effect is modest."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The texts disagree about the size of the calorie effect. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bea42e3043","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-311","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising taxes on sugary drinks will reduce consumption, because higher prices discourage buyers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>After one city added a tax on sugary drinks, sales within the city fell sharply. However, sales at stores just outside the city limits rose, as some shoppers crossed the border to buy cheaper drinks. Overall consumption among residents dropped, but by less than the in-city sales figures alone suggested.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, in-city sales figures alone would most likely have","choices":["exaggerated how much residents' consumption fell.","underestimated how much residents' consumption fell.","shown that sales rose.","matched overall consumption exactly."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Some purchases shifted outside the city. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7288447880","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-311","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising taxes on sugary drinks will reduce consumption, because higher prices discourage buyers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>After one city added a tax on sugary drinks, sales within the city fell sharply. However, sales at stores just outside the city limits rose, as some shoppers crossed the border to buy cheaper drinks. Overall consumption among residents dropped, but by less than the in-city sales figures alone suggested.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions stores \"just outside the city limits\" mainly to","choices":["show that the tax applied to the entire region.","argue that stores outside the city should be closed.","explain why the drop in in-city sales overstated the tax's effect.","describe where the city's sugary drinks are made."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cross-border purchases partly offset the decline. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.56584db2f4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-312","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people assume that the Middle Ages were a period in which scientific thinking came to a halt in Europe.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Historians of science point out that medieval scholars developed new theories of motion, studied optics, and built mechanical clocks. Universities founded during the period became centers for debating natural philosophy. While some ancient texts were lost in Europe, others were preserved, translated, and expanded upon, often through contact with scholars in the Islamic world.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By presenting examples of scientific and scholarly activity during the period to challenge that assumption","By claiming that universities were closed during the Middle Ages","By arguing that no ancient texts survived","By agreeing that scientific thinking stopped entirely"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 lists theories of motion, optics, clocks, and universities. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.95943b5e13","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-313","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Proponents of year-round schooling argue that the long summer break causes students to forget much of what they learned, forcing teachers to spend weeks reviewing each fall.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of year-round calendars, which spread the same number of school days across shorter breaks, have found only small differences in overall achievement compared with traditional calendars. Some evidence suggests that summer learning loss is largest among students from low-income families, who may have fewer educational opportunities during the break.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that year-round schooling greatly improves achievement for all students","By suggesting that year-round calendars show only small overall benefits, though summer loss may affect some students more than others","By arguing that schools should add more days to the calendar","By claiming that students forget nothing over the summer"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 finds small differences but notes uneven summer loss. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.59cf357670","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-314","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some gardeners insist that talking to plants helps them grow, pointing to their own flourishing gardens as proof.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A few experiments have reported that plants exposed to recorded speech grew slightly faster, but these studies were small and often poorly controlled. Gardeners who talk to their plants may also water, prune, and check them more often, and that extra attention could explain healthier growth.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that gardeners should stop watering their plants","By claiming that talking to plants harms them","By suggesting that other forms of care provided by these gardeners might explain their plants' growth","By agreeing that speech is the main reason plants grow"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 points to extra watering and pruning as an alternative explanation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1848645284","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-314","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some gardeners insist that talking to plants helps them grow, pointing to their own flourishing gardens as proof.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A few experiments have reported that plants exposed to recorded speech grew slightly faster, but these studies were small and often poorly controlled. Gardeners who talk to their plants may also water, prune, and check them more often, and that extra attention could explain healthier growth.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the experiments reporting faster growth are weak evidence because they","choices":["used real plants instead of models.","were small and often poorly controlled.","lasted too long.","were conducted by gardeners."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"These studies were small and often poorly controlled.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a7771f1255","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-314","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some gardeners insist that talking to plants helps them grow, pointing to their own flourishing gardens as proof.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A few experiments have reported that plants exposed to recorded speech grew slightly faster, but these studies were small and often poorly controlled. Gardeners who talk to their plants may also water, prune, and check them more often, and that extra attention could explain healthier growth.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 describes an unrelated gardening method.","Text 2 agrees completely with Text 1.","Text 2 provides new evidence supporting Text 1.","Text 2 offers an alternative explanation for the evidence cited in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Healthy gardens may result from extra care, not talking. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.60e43a8a39","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-315","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Museum curators have traditionally argued that fragile artifacts should never be handled by visitors, since even careful touching can cause gradual damage.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some museums now use high-quality replicas, created from detailed scans, that visitors are encouraged to touch. Surveys suggest that handling replicas helps visitors understand an object's weight and texture, and the originals remain safely behind glass. Curators who once opposed hands-on displays have found that replicas address their main concern.</p>","prompt":"The curators described in Text 2 would most likely say that replicas","choices":["address the concern expressed in Text 1.","should never be touched.","cause more damage than originals.","are less accurate than photographs."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Replicas \"address their main concern.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d5056fc0b2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-316","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that building more highway lanes is the best way to relieve traffic congestion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of many cities have found that adding highway capacity often leads to more driving. When roads become less crowded, people take trips they previously avoided, choose to live farther away, or shift from transit to cars. Within a few years, traffic frequently returns to its previous levels, a pattern known as induced demand.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that added lanes often encourage more driving, so congestion tends to return","By suggesting that cities should build no roads at all","By claiming that fewer people drive when highways expand","By agreeing that new lanes permanently solve congestion"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 describes induced demand. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.747d3c1462","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-316","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that building more highway lanes is the best way to relieve traffic congestion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of many cities have found that adding highway capacity often leads to more driving. When roads become less crowded, people take trips they previously avoided, choose to live farther away, or shift from transit to cars. Within a few years, traffic frequently returns to its previous levels, a pattern known as induced demand.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which situation is an example of induced demand?","choices":["After a highway is widened, more people decide to commute by car from distant suburbs.","After a highway is widened, fewer people drive.","After a highway closes, people stop traveling.","After a bus line is added, traffic decreases."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Less crowded roads encourage new driving trips. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.17d46e2821","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-317","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many writers claim that people have become worse at paying attention because of smartphones, citing a widely repeated claim that human attention spans are now shorter than a goldfish's.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Researchers who have searched for the source of the goldfish comparison have been unable to find any study supporting it. Attention also varies greatly by task; people who struggle to focus on a dull lecture may concentrate for hours on a game or a novel. Whether smartphones affect attention is a legitimate question, but the goldfish claim is not evidence.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the example of people concentrating for hours on a game suggests that","choices":["lectures are more engaging than games.","attention depends on the task, not a single fixed span.","games damage attention.","people cannot focus for long periods."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Attention also varies greatly by task.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ee755ec67a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-318","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some architects argue that tall glass skyscrapers are energy-efficient because they let in abundant daylight, reducing the need for electric lighting.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Glass walls do admit daylight, but they also let heat escape in winter and absorb heat in summer, often requiring powerful heating and cooling systems. Studies of office towers have found that fully glazed buildings frequently use more total energy than buildings with a mix of windows and insulated walls, even after lighting savings are counted.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, buildings with a mix of windows and insulated walls tend to","choices":["receive no daylight.","require no heating or cooling.","use more lighting energy than glass buildings and more total energy.","use less total energy than fully glazed buildings."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Fully glazed buildings \"frequently use more total energy.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.53ccf08ccc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-319","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many coaches have long believed that athletes should stretch before exercise to prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Several large studies of runners and military recruits found that stretching before exercise did little to reduce injury rates. Some research suggests that a dynamic warm-up, such as light jogging and movement drills, prepares muscles more effectively than holding static stretches. Stretching may still improve flexibility over time when done regularly.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that static stretching prevents most injuries","By arguing that flexibility has no value","By presenting evidence that pre-exercise stretching does little to prevent injuries and suggesting a different warm-up","By claiming that warming up increases injuries"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Studies found little reduction in injuries; dynamic warm-ups may work better. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.01bf0aa0b3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-319","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many coaches have long believed that athletes should stretch before exercise to prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Several large studies of runners and military recruits found that stretching before exercise did little to reduce injury rates. Some research suggests that a dynamic warm-up, such as light jogging and movement drills, prepares muscles more effectively than holding static stretches. Stretching may still improve flexibility over time when done regularly.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which statement about stretching is most accurate?","choices":["It should be done only after injuries occur.","It is more effective than any warm-up for preventing injuries.","It may improve flexibility over time, even if it does little to prevent injuries before exercise.","It has no effects of any kind."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 grants a flexibility benefit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8dd4b7d47c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-319","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many coaches have long believed that athletes should stretch before exercise to prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Several large studies of runners and military recruits found that stretching before exercise did little to reduce injury rates. Some research suggests that a dynamic warm-up, such as light jogging and movement drills, prepares muscles more effectively than holding static stretches. Stretching may still improve flexibility over time when done regularly.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 confirms the belief described in Text 1.","Text 2 challenges a long-held belief described in Text 1.","Text 2 and Text 1 discuss unrelated sports.","Text 2 describes the history of coaching."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Evidence contradicts the traditional practice. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6acb7b2e1b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-320","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have argued that a small ancient kingdom collapsed suddenly because of a single devastating invasion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at the kingdom's cities reveal a more gradual process. Layers of soil show declining harvests over several decades, and records mention repeated droughts and rising grain prices. The invasion occurred, but it struck a kingdom already weakened by years of hardship.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which evidence suggests a gradual decline?","choices":["Soil layers showing declining harvests over decades","A single record of the invasion","Weapons found in one city","A description of the kingdom's founding"],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Declining harvests over several decades.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e24b99d049","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-322","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that lightning never strikes the same place twice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Tall structures and high points in a landscape are struck repeatedly. Some skyscrapers are hit dozens of times each year, and lightning rods are designed precisely because tall objects attract strikes again and again. The saying may be meant as encouragement, but it is not accurate.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that lightning rods attract only one strike","By pointing out that lightning frequently strikes the same tall objects repeatedly","By agreeing that lightning never strikes one place twice","By claiming that skyscrapers are never struck by lightning"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Skyscrapers are hit many times. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3fe734e4ae","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-323","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of organic farming argue that it is better for the environment in every respect than conventional farming.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Organic farms often support greater biodiversity and use fewer synthetic pesticides. However, they typically produce smaller harvests per acre, which means more land may be needed to grow the same amount of food. Whether organic farming is better overall can depend on how much land is available and which environmental impacts are measured.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging environmental benefits of organic farming while noting drawbacks that make its overall impact more complicated","By agreeing that organic farming is better in every respect","By arguing that biodiversity does not matter","By claiming that organic farms use more synthetic pesticides"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 cites benefits and lower yields. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7931855a4a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-323","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of organic farming argue that it is better for the environment in every respect than conventional farming.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Organic farms often support greater biodiversity and use fewer synthetic pesticides. However, they typically produce smaller harvests per acre, which means more land may be needed to grow the same amount of food. Whether organic farming is better overall can depend on how much land is available and which environmental impacts are measured.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely object to the word \"every\" in Text 1 because","choices":["organic farming has no environmental benefits.","conventional farming is always better.","organic farming has some environmental disadvantages as well as advantages.","the author did not study organic farming."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Lower yields show it is not better in every respect. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c2684aaebb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-325","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>A popular idea holds that people are either \"left-brained,\" meaning logical and analytical, or \"right-brained,\" meaning creative and intuitive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging studies show that both hemispheres are active during nearly all complex tasks, including math and art. Some functions, such as aspects of language, rely more on one side, but researchers have found no evidence that individuals consistently use one hemisphere more than the other.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that","choices":["math activates only the left hemisphere.","individuals consistently use one hemisphere more.","some functions rely more heavily on one hemisphere.","art activates only the right hemisphere."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Aspects of language rely more on one side.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1ac7557286","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-325","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>A popular idea holds that people are either \"left-brained,\" meaning logical and analytical, or \"right-brained,\" meaning creative and intuitive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging studies show that both hemispheres are active during nearly all complex tasks, including math and art. Some functions, such as aspects of language, rely more on one side, but researchers have found no evidence that individuals consistently use one hemisphere more than the other.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 describes a different theory of creativity.","Text 2 expands on the popular idea in Text 1.","Text 2 uses research to reject the popular idea in Text 1.","Text 2 argues that the idea in Text 1 is proven."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 finds no evidence for hemispheric types. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.180eb06ae3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-326","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video games are a waste of time with no educational value.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on certain action games has found that players can improve in tracking multiple moving objects and switching attention quickly. Puzzle and strategy games may encourage planning and problem solving. The benefits depend heavily on the type of game, and researchers caution that many popular games have not been shown to teach specific skills.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, action games have been linked to improvements in","choices":["tracking multiple moving objects.","spelling.","memorizing historical dates.","learning foreign languages."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Players \"improve in tracking multiple moving objects.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c49805c234","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-326","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video games are a waste of time with no educational value.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on certain action games has found that players can improve in tracking multiple moving objects and switching attention quickly. Puzzle and strategy games may encourage planning and problem solving. The benefits depend heavily on the type of game, and researchers caution that many popular games have not been shown to teach specific skills.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the critics' claim is","choices":["irrelevant to research on games.","correct about puzzle games only.","too broad, because some games may have educational value.","fully supported by research."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Some games show benefits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8a535c4871","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-328","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that bats are blind.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>All bat species can see, and some large fruit bats have excellent vision suited to finding food at dusk. Many smaller bats rely heavily on echolocation, emitting sounds and listening to echoes to navigate and hunt in darkness, which may be why people assume bats cannot see.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, large fruit bats are notable because they","choices":["rely entirely on echolocation.","cannot see at all.","have excellent vision.","hunt insects underwater."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Some large fruit bats have excellent vision.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ea68423e59","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-329","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some business leaders argue that open-plan offices, without walls or private offices, encourage collaboration by making it easy for employees to talk.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A study that tracked employees before and after two companies switched to open-plan offices found that face-to-face interaction actually decreased by about seventy percent, while email and messaging increased. Researchers suggest that employees, lacking privacy, may have used headphones and digital messages to avoid being overheard or interrupted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 describes the architecture of office buildings.","Text 2 summarizes Text 1 without adding evidence.","Text 2 confirms the expectation in Text 1.","Text 2 reports a result contrary to the expectation in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Collaboration was expected to rise but face-to-face contact fell. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.cd36a818f8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-330","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some conservation groups argue that zoos mainly serve entertainment and do little for wild animal populations.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many accredited zoos run breeding programs for endangered species, and several species that had vanished from the wild have been reintroduced from zoo-bred populations. Zoos also fund field research and habitat protection. Critics are right that not all zoos meet high standards, but well-run zoos can make meaningful contributions to conservation.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that well-run zoos contribute to conservation through breeding, research, and habitat protection, while conceding that some zoos fall short","By suggesting that endangered species should never be bred in captivity","By agreeing that zoos do nothing for wild populations","By claiming that all zoos meet high standards"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 cites breeding and funding while granting that not all zoos are good. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0e77fda8f4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-330","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some conservation groups argue that zoos mainly serve entertainment and do little for wild animal populations.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many accredited zoos run breeding programs for endangered species, and several species that had vanished from the wild have been reintroduced from zoo-bred populations. Zoos also fund field research and habitat protection. Critics are right that not all zoos meet high standards, but well-run zoos can make meaningful contributions to conservation.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the conservation groups in Text 1 that","choices":["some zoos do not meet high standards.","breeding programs have failed.","zoos serve only as entertainment.","habitat protection is unnecessary."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Critics are right that not all zoos meet high standards.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6e4dcde058","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-331","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some technology writers predict that printed books will soon disappear, replaced entirely by e-books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>E-book sales grew rapidly for several years after e-readers were introduced, but they later leveled off, and print books continue to account for a large share of sales. Surveys suggest that many readers value the physical experience of print, while others prefer e-books for travel. The two formats appear likely to coexist.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, some readers prefer e-books because they","choices":["cost more than print books.","offer a physical reading experience.","cannot be read on e-readers.","are convenient for travel."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Others prefer e-books for travel.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b84c69b0b6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-331","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some technology writers predict that printed books will soon disappear, replaced entirely by e-books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>E-book sales grew rapidly for several years after e-readers were introduced, but they later leveled off, and print books continue to account for a large share of sales. Surveys suggest that many readers value the physical experience of print, while others prefer e-books for travel. The two formats appear likely to coexist.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 supports the prediction in Text 1.","Text 2 agrees that print sales have ended.","Text 2 discusses the history of printing.","Text 2 presents evidence against the prediction in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sales leveled off and print endures. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c2bd5e0235","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-332","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that sugar makes children hyperactive, and parents often report that their children become wild after eating sweets.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In controlled experiments, children were given either sugar or a sugar-free substitute without anyone knowing which. Researchers found no difference in behavior. In one study, parents who were told their child had eaten sugar rated the child as more hyperactive, even when the child had received the substitute, suggesting that expectations may shape what parents notice.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that controlled studies found no effect and that parents' expectations may explain their observations","By agreeing that sugar causes hyperactivity","By claiming that sugar substitutes cause hyperactivity","By suggesting that parents cannot observe their children"],"answer":"A","explanation":"No behavioral difference; expectation influences ratings. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c340515836","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-332","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that sugar makes children hyperactive, and parents often report that their children become wild after eating sweets.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In controlled experiments, children were given either sugar or a sugar-free substitute without anyone knowing which. Researchers found no difference in behavior. In one study, parents who were told their child had eaten sugar rated the child as more hyperactive, even when the child had received the substitute, suggesting that expectations may shape what parents notice.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, parents who were told their child had eaten sugar","choices":["rated the child as more hyperactive even when no sugar had been eaten.","gave their child more sugar.","refused to rate their child's behavior.","rated the child as calmer."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Expectation led to higher ratings. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.be6628b8f8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-333","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some farmers argue that bees are the only pollinators that matter for crops.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees and wild bees are important, but studies of crop fields around the world show that flies, beetles, moths, and butterflies also pollinate many crops. In some fields, these insects account for a substantial share of flower visits. Protecting a variety of pollinators may make crop production more resilient if bee populations decline.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the farmers in Text 1 that","choices":["pollination is unimportant for crops.","only bees visit crop flowers.","bees are important pollinators.","beetles are the most important pollinators."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Honeybees and wild bees are important.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d681645e35","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-335","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have credited a single general's brilliant strategy for an army's victory in a famous battle.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Letters and supply records from the campaign reveal that the victorious army had far better access to food and ammunition than its opponent, whose supply lines had been disrupted by weeks of heavy rain. The general's decisions mattered, but historians who emphasize only strategy may overlook the practical conditions that made victory possible.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that strategy alone determined the outcome","By arguing that supply advantages and weather also contributed significantly to the victory","By claiming that the general made no decisions during the battle","By suggesting that the battle never took place"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Supplies and disrupted enemy lines mattered too. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0f9c17e62e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-337","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many runners believe that expensive, highly cushioned running shoes prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of runners wearing different kinds of shoes have not consistently found that cushioning reduces injury rates. Some evidence suggests that choosing a shoe that feels comfortable to the individual runner may matter more than the amount of cushioning or the price.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that evidence does not clearly show that cushioning prevents injuries and that comfort may matter more","By agreeing that expensive shoes always prevent injuries","By suggesting that runners should run barefoot","By claiming that cushioning causes injuries"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Studies are inconsistent; comfort may matter most. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.342ff1d26b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-337","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many runners believe that expensive, highly cushioned running shoes prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of runners wearing different kinds of shoes have not consistently found that cushioning reduces injury rates. Some evidence suggests that choosing a shoe that feels comfortable to the individual runner may matter more than the amount of cushioning or the price.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a runner trying to reduce injury risk would most likely be advised to","choices":["buy the most expensive shoes available.","choose shoes that feel comfortable to them.","avoid wearing shoes.","select the most cushioned shoes."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Comfort \"may matter more.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a14e26de60","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-338","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban designers argue that pedestrian-only shopping streets always attract more customers than streets open to cars.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many pedestrian streets have thrived, especially in dense city centers with heavy foot traffic. But in some smaller towns, closing main streets to cars reduced visits, as shoppers who arrived by car found parking too distant. Several of these towns later reopened their streets to traffic. Success appears to depend on local conditions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that pedestrian streets always attract more customers","By claiming that no pedestrian street has ever succeeded","By suggesting that all towns should close streets to cars","By arguing that pedestrian streets succeed in some places but not others, depending on local conditions"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Dense centers thrive; some small towns struggled. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.2141e7a4bb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-338","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban designers argue that pedestrian-only shopping streets always attract more customers than streets open to cars.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many pedestrian streets have thrived, especially in dense city centers with heavy foot traffic. But in some smaller towns, closing main streets to cars reduced visits, as shoppers who arrived by car found parking too distant. Several of these towns later reopened their streets to traffic. Success appears to depend on local conditions.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, some small towns reopened streets to traffic most likely because","choices":["the streets were too narrow for walking.","foot traffic increased too much.","pedestrians demanded more parking lots.","closing them to cars had reduced shopper visits."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Visits declined when parking was distant. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5caac3ac1f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-338","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban designers argue that pedestrian-only shopping streets always attract more customers than streets open to cars.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many pedestrian streets have thrived, especially in dense city centers with heavy foot traffic. But in some smaller towns, closing main streets to cars reduced visits, as shoppers who arrived by car found parking too distant. Several of these towns later reopened their streets to traffic. Success appears to depend on local conditions.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely object to the word \"always\" in Text 1 because","choices":["cars are banned in every city center.","pedestrian streets never attract customers.","all towns have heavy foot traffic.","pedestrian streets have not succeeded in every setting."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Some towns saw fewer visits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d9a720986f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-339","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have argued that dinosaurs were wiped out slowly by gradual climate change over millions of years.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A layer of rock found around the world marks the end of the age of dinosaurs. It contains unusually high levels of iridium, an element rare on Earth but common in asteroids, and a large impact crater of the right age has been identified. Many scientists conclude that an asteroid impact caused a rapid mass extinction, though some argue that volcanic eruptions had already stressed ecosystems.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that the extinction happened only through gradual climate change","By presenting evidence pointing to a sudden asteroid impact, while noting that other factors may also have played a role","By claiming that dinosaurs survived the asteroid impact","By arguing that iridium is common on Earth"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Iridium layer and crater support a rapid event. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e2147804df","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-339","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have argued that dinosaurs were wiped out slowly by gradual climate change over millions of years.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A layer of rock found around the world marks the end of the age of dinosaurs. It contains unusually high levels of iridium, an element rare on Earth but common in asteroids, and a large impact crater of the right age has been identified. Many scientists conclude that an asteroid impact caused a rapid mass extinction, though some argue that volcanic eruptions had already stressed ecosystems.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the iridium in the rock layer is significant because","choices":["iridium is found only in dinosaur bones.","iridium is common in asteroids but rare on Earth.","iridium forms slowly over millions of years.","iridium causes volcanic eruptions."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It suggests an extraterrestrial source. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3f94367eb8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-339","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have argued that dinosaurs were wiped out slowly by gradual climate change over millions of years.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A layer of rock found around the world marks the end of the age of dinosaurs. It contains unusually high levels of iridium, an element rare on Earth but common in asteroids, and a large impact crater of the right age has been identified. Many scientists conclude that an asteroid impact caused a rapid mass extinction, though some argue that volcanic eruptions had already stressed ecosystems.</p>","prompt":"The mention of volcanic eruptions in Text 2 suggests that","choices":["the author rejects the asteroid explanation.","the asteroid never struck Earth.","volcanoes produce iridium layers.","the extinction may have had more than one contributing cause."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Volcanoes \"had already stressed ecosystems.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a803c72a1e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-340","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people believe that reading on screens damages eyesight permanently.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Long periods of screen use can cause temporary eye strain, dryness, and blurred vision, often because people blink less while staring at screens. Eye doctors recommend regular breaks, such as looking at something distant every twenty minutes. There is little evidence, however, that screen use by itself causes permanent damage to adult vision.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, screen use contributes to eye strain partly because people","choices":["take too many breaks.","sit too far from screens.","blink less while looking at screens.","read printed books too often."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"People blink less while staring at screens.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f2e4d198e3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-340","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people believe that reading on screens damages eyesight permanently.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Long periods of screen use can cause temporary eye strain, dryness, and blurred vision, often because people blink less while staring at screens. Eye doctors recommend regular breaks, such as looking at something distant every twenty minutes. There is little evidence, however, that screen use by itself causes permanent damage to adult vision.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely recommend that someone working at a computer all day","choices":["avoid blinking.","use screens without breaks.","stop using computers entirely.","look at distant objects periodically."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Regular breaks looking at distant objects. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.add45b0e31","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-341","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that automation will eliminate most jobs within a few decades.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Past waves of automation, such as the mechanization of farming, eliminated many specific jobs but also created new types of work, from factory employment to services. Automation can cause serious hardship for workers whose skills become obsolete, but history suggests that total employment has tended to recover as economies adapt, though often slowly and unevenly.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that automation never causes hardship","By suggesting that farming was never mechanized","By arguing that automation historically created new work even as it eliminated some jobs, though transitions can be difficult","By agreeing that automation will eliminate most jobs"],"answer":"C","explanation":"History shows job loss and creation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.25fb3fa403","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-341","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that automation will eliminate most jobs within a few decades.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Past waves of automation, such as the mechanization of farming, eliminated many specific jobs but also created new types of work, from factory employment to services. Automation can cause serious hardship for workers whose skills become obsolete, but history suggests that total employment has tended to recover as economies adapt, though often slowly and unevenly.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions the mechanization of farming mainly to","choices":["explain how factories were built.","argue that farming should not be mechanized.","provide a historical example of automation's mixed effects on work.","show that farmers opposed machines."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It eliminated some jobs and was followed by new types of work. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9a2c740e28","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-342","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some coaches argue that young athletes should specialize in a single sport early to develop elite skills.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of college and professional athletes found that many played several sports during childhood and specialized only in their teens. Early specialization has been linked to higher rates of overuse injuries and burnout, while playing multiple sports may develop a broader range of physical skills.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 supports the recommendation in Text 1.","Text 2 agrees but adds that coaches should train more.","Text 2 discusses professional contracts.","Text 2 challenges the recommendation in Text 1 with evidence about athletes' development."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Evidence counters early specialization. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f8aaefaa85","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-344","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some residents oppose building apartment complexes in their neighborhoods, arguing that new housing will lower nearby property values.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of several cities found that new apartment buildings generally had little effect on nearby home values, and in some cases values rose slightly as new residents supported local shops and services. Effects varied, but the fear of widespread declines was not supported by most of the evidence.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that shops closed near new apartments","By agreeing that new apartments always lower property values","By citing studies showing that new apartments usually had little negative effect on nearby property values","By arguing that no new housing should be built"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Little effect, sometimes slight increases. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.65c9b224f9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-344","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some residents oppose building apartment complexes in their neighborhoods, arguing that new housing will lower nearby property values.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of several cities found that new apartment buildings generally had little effect on nearby home values, and in some cases values rose slightly as new residents supported local shops and services. Effects varied, but the fear of widespread declines was not supported by most of the evidence.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that","choices":["property values fell everywhere.","no studies have been done.","effects of new apartment buildings varied.","all residents support new housing."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Effects varied.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6ce0f12621","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-345","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that dogs feel guilty when they misbehave, pointing to the \"guilty look\" dogs show when owners discover a mess.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one experiment, owners left the room, and researchers either had the dog eat a forbidden treat or removed it themselves. Owners were then told, sometimes falsely, that their dog had misbehaved. Dogs showed the \"guilty look\" most often when owners scolded them, regardless of whether they had actually eaten the treat, suggesting the look responds to the owner's behavior rather than to guilt.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that dogs cannot eat treats","By arguing that the \"guilty look\" appears in response to owners' scolding, not necessarily because dogs misbehaved","By claiming that dogs never react to scolding","By agreeing that dogs feel guilt after misbehaving"],"answer":"B","explanation":"The look appeared when scolded, regardless of actual behavior. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e7bbabd0a7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-345","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that dogs feel guilty when they misbehave, pointing to the \"guilty look\" dogs show when owners discover a mess.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one experiment, owners left the room, and researchers either had the dog eat a forbidden treat or removed it themselves. Owners were then told, sometimes falsely, that their dog had misbehaved. Dogs showed the \"guilty look\" most often when owners scolded them, regardless of whether they had actually eaten the treat, suggesting the look responds to the owner's behavior rather than to guilt.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the experiment included dogs whose treats were removed by researchers in order to","choices":["make the dogs hungry.","test the owners' memories.","compare dogs that misbehaved with dogs that did not.","train the dogs to obey."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It separated actual misbehavior from scolding. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5058b2ae35","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-345","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that dogs feel guilty when they misbehave, pointing to the \"guilty look\" dogs show when owners discover a mess.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one experiment, owners left the room, and researchers either had the dog eat a forbidden treat or removed it themselves. Owners were then told, sometimes falsely, that their dog had misbehaved. Dogs showed the \"guilty look\" most often when owners scolded them, regardless of whether they had actually eaten the treat, suggesting the look responds to the owner's behavior rather than to guilt.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 restates Text 1.","Text 2 offers evidence for a different explanation of the behavior described in Text 1.","Text 2 describes a different animal.","Text 2 confirms the explanation in Text 1."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The look reflects owners' behavior, not guilt. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.faf61b0449","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-346","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people think that the Great Wall of China was built all at once as a single continuous structure.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The walls known collectively as the Great Wall were built, rebuilt, and extended by different dynasties over roughly two thousand years. Many sections are separate walls rather than one continuous line, and they were constructed from local materials ranging from rammed earth to stone and brick.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that the wall was built all at once","By explaining that the walls were built in many phases by different dynasties and do not form one continuous structure","By claiming that the wall is made entirely of brick","By arguing that the wall was never built"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Built over two thousand years in separate sections. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.87805a0ab0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-346","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people think that the Great Wall of China was built all at once as a single continuous structure.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The walls known collectively as the Great Wall were built, rebuilt, and extended by different dynasties over roughly two thousand years. Many sections are separate walls rather than one continuous line, and they were constructed from local materials ranging from rammed earth to stone and brick.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 responds to the belief in Text 1?","choices":["It supports the belief with archaeological evidence.","It ignores the belief entirely.","It argues that the wall is a single brick structure.","It corrects the belief with historical information about how the walls were built."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Many dynasties, separate sections. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b6074570cf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-347","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children who watch educational television learn as much as children who read books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of young children suggest that well-designed educational programs can teach vocabulary and early math concepts. However, children tend to learn more when adults watch with them and discuss what they see, and shared book reading exposes children to a wider range of vocabulary than most television programs do.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that children learn nothing from television","By suggesting that adults should never watch television with children","By agreeing that television teaches exactly as much as books","By acknowledging that educational programs can teach some skills while suggesting that shared reading offers additional benefits"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Programs help, but reading exposes children to more vocabulary. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f2e8d50ac1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-347","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children who watch educational television learn as much as children who read books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of young children suggest that well-designed educational programs can teach vocabulary and early math concepts. However, children tend to learn more when adults watch with them and discuss what they see, and shared book reading exposes children to a wider range of vocabulary than most television programs do.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the parents in Text 1 that","choices":["television is better than reading.","educational television can help children learn.","children should avoid educational programs.","books have no educational value."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Programs \"can teach vocabulary and early math concepts.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9cc99044b4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-348","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have suggested that humans can survive on Mars in the near future by building domed cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Mars presents major challenges beyond breathable air: its thin atmosphere provides little protection from radiation, temperatures swing drastically, and dust storms can last for weeks. Engineers are studying underground habitats and radiation shielding, but many researchers believe that long-term settlement will require technologies that do not yet exist.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that humans should never study Mars","By claiming that Mars has a thick atmosphere","By agreeing that domed cities will solve every challenge on Mars","By arguing that Mars poses challenges, such as radiation and dust storms, that may require technologies not yet developed"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Radiation, temperature swings, and dust require new technology. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.efec04d012","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-349","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art critics dismiss street art as vandalism with no artistic merit.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Unauthorized graffiti can damage property, and many cities prohibit it. Yet some street artists create commissioned murals that neighborhoods welcome, and several museums have exhibited works by artists who began on the street. Whether a piece counts as vandalism or art may depend on permission, context, and community response.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the critics in Text 1 that","choices":["all murals are vandalism.","unauthorized graffiti can damage property.","street artists never receive commissions.","museums should never show street art."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Unauthorized graffiti can damage property.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4100b312aa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-349","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art critics dismiss street art as vandalism with no artistic merit.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Unauthorized graffiti can damage property, and many cities prohibit it. Yet some street artists create commissioned murals that neighborhoods welcome, and several museums have exhibited works by artists who began on the street. Whether a piece counts as vandalism or art may depend on permission, context, and community response.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, commissioned murals differ from vandalism mainly because they","choices":["are created with permission and welcomed by communities.","are always painted indoors.","are created only by museum employees.","use different kinds of paint."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Permission and community response matter. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.88e9b00889","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-350","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that students learn best when teachers explain concepts directly before students practice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Other educators favor having students attempt a problem before receiving instruction. Studies suggest that struggling with a problem first can help students notice what they do not understand, making the later explanation more meaningful. However, this approach works best when students have enough background knowledge to make progress; complete beginners may simply become frustrated.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that direct explanation should always come first for every student","By claiming that instruction is unnecessary","By arguing that frustration always improves learning","By suggesting that attempting problems before instruction can help, though only when students have sufficient background knowledge"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Productive struggle helps, with a caveat for beginners. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6bcf90e2c4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-351","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some ecologists argue that all non-native species should be removed from protected areas.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many non-native species cause serious harm, outcompeting native plants and animals. But some have become integrated into ecosystems in ways that benefit native wildlife; certain non-native shrubs, for example, now provide nesting sites for endangered birds where native vegetation has disappeared. Managers may need to evaluate each species' effects rather than applying a single rule.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that while many non-native species are harmful, some benefit native wildlife and should be evaluated individually","By suggesting that endangered birds prefer native shrubs only","By claiming that non-native species never cause harm","By agreeing that every non-native species must be removed"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Some non-natives help; case-by-case evaluation is needed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a6c09690e6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-351","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some ecologists argue that all non-native species should be removed from protected areas.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many non-native species cause serious harm, outcompeting native plants and animals. But some have become integrated into ecosystems in ways that benefit native wildlife; certain non-native shrubs, for example, now provide nesting sites for endangered birds where native vegetation has disappeared. Managers may need to evaluate each species' effects rather than applying a single rule.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions non-native shrubs mainly to","choices":["argue that birds should be relocated.","show that shrubs outcompete all native plants.","give an example of a non-native species that benefits native wildlife.","explain how shrubs were introduced."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They provide nesting sites for endangered birds. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.aea1932a02","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-351","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some ecologists argue that all non-native species should be removed from protected areas.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many non-native species cause serious harm, outcompeting native plants and animals. But some have become integrated into ecosystems in ways that benefit native wildlife; certain non-native shrubs, for example, now provide nesting sites for endangered birds where native vegetation has disappeared. Managers may need to evaluate each species' effects rather than applying a single rule.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the ecologists in Text 1 that","choices":["protected areas need no management.","native vegetation is unimportant.","many non-native species can harm ecosystems.","no non-native species are harmful."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Many non-native species cause serious harm.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.45fc95b626","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-352","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that drinking eight glasses of water a day is necessary for good health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The body's water needs vary with climate, activity, and body size, and much of the water people need comes from food and other beverages. Most healthy adults can rely on thirst to guide how much they drink. The eight-glass rule may be a useful reminder for some people but is not a scientific requirement for everyone.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that water needs vary and that the eight-glass rule is not a universal requirement","By claiming that people need no water","By suggesting that food contains no water","By agreeing that everyone must drink exactly eight glasses"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Needs vary; thirst is usually a reliable guide. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.88ccbecee7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-352","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that drinking eight glasses of water a day is necessary for good health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The body's water needs vary with climate, activity, and body size, and much of the water people need comes from food and other beverages. Most healthy adults can rely on thirst to guide how much they drink. The eight-glass rule may be a useful reminder for some people but is not a scientific requirement for everyone.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, much of the water people need comes from","choices":["food and other beverages.","sweat.","only plain water.","medical supplements."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Much of the water people need comes from food and other beverages.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e9cb599e19","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-353","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that the invention of the printing press immediately transformed European society.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Printing did make books cheaper and more widely available, but literacy rates rose slowly, and for decades most printed works were religious texts and classical writings already familiar to scholars. The press's broader social effects unfolded over generations as schooling expanded and new kinds of writing, such as newspapers, developed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that society changed overnight","By arguing that the printing press's effects on society developed gradually rather than immediately","By claiming that printing made books more expensive","By suggesting that the printing press was never used"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Effects \"unfolded over generations.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6f5cb5acf6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-353","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that the invention of the printing press immediately transformed European society.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Printing did make books cheaper and more widely available, but literacy rates rose slowly, and for decades most printed works were religious texts and classical writings already familiar to scholars. The press's broader social effects unfolded over generations as schooling expanded and new kinds of writing, such as newspapers, developed.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the historians in Text 1 that the printing press","choices":["made books more widely available.","was invented after newspapers.","reduced literacy.","had no effect on society."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Printing did make books cheaper and more widely available.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a9e0b5d4ae","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-354","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some psychologists argue that people make better decisions when they carefully analyze every option.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>For complex choices with many factors, such as choosing an apartment, some studies have found that people who thought carefully and then took a break before deciding made choices they were later more satisfied with. For simple decisions, careful analysis works well. Deliberation is valuable, but the best approach may depend on the type of decision.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, people deciding among many complex options were more satisfied when they","choices":["analyzed only one factor.","let someone else decide.","thought carefully and then took a break before deciding.","decided instantly without thinking."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They \"thought carefully and then took a break.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.55b3380017","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-354","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some psychologists argue that people make better decisions when they carefully analyze every option.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>For complex choices with many factors, such as choosing an apartment, some studies have found that people who thought carefully and then took a break before deciding made choices they were later more satisfied with. For simple decisions, careful analysis works well. Deliberation is valuable, but the best approach may depend on the type of decision.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the psychologists in Text 1 that","choices":["analysis always leads to worse decisions.","breaks are harmful.","all decisions are simple.","deliberation has value."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Deliberation is valuable.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ad0880f039","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-355","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that goldfish have memories lasting only a few seconds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments have shown that goldfish can be trained to respond to sounds or colors associated with feeding and can remember these associations for months. Some goldfish have learned to push levers for food at particular times of day. Their memory is far better than the popular belief suggests.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that goldfish forget everything within seconds","By claiming that goldfish cannot learn","By arguing that goldfish do not respond to food","By presenting experimental evidence that goldfish can remember associations for months"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Training experiments show long-lasting memory. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e9e50fba45","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-355","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that goldfish have memories lasting only a few seconds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments have shown that goldfish can be trained to respond to sounds or colors associated with feeding and can remember these associations for months. Some goldfish have learned to push levers for food at particular times of day. Their memory is far better than the popular belief suggests.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?","choices":["It uses evidence to contradict the popular belief in Text 1.","It discusses the memories of dogs.","It restates Text 1 without evidence.","It supports the belief in Text 1."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Memory lasts months, not seconds. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f7ca1c54a0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-356","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people learn best when information matches their preferred \"learning style,\" such as visual or auditory.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies that tested students' preferred styles and then taught them in matching or mismatching ways found little difference in learning. Students may prefer certain formats, but most people learn more from combining words, images, and active practice than from a single mode. Researchers suggest that the content being taught, not an individual's supposed style, should guide instruction.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that teaching to learning styles greatly improves learning","By arguing that matching instruction to preferred styles does not improve learning and that mixed approaches are generally more effective","By claiming that students have no preferences for formats","By arguing that images should never be used in teaching"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Studies found little difference; combining modes helps more. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ccc60ea42c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-356","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people learn best when information matches their preferred \"learning style,\" such as visual or auditory.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies that tested students' preferred styles and then taught them in matching or mismatching ways found little difference in learning. Students may prefer certain formats, but most people learn more from combining words, images, and active practice than from a single mode. Researchers suggest that the content being taught, not an individual's supposed style, should guide instruction.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that","choices":["learning styles determine how much students learn.","auditory learners cannot learn from images.","students may prefer certain formats of information.","instruction should use only one mode."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Students may prefer certain formats.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ce767e80db","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-357","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some city officials argue that increasing police patrols is the most effective way to reduce crime in neighborhoods.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research in several cities has found that improving street lighting and cleaning up vacant lots were associated with reductions in certain crimes, sometimes at lower cost than additional patrols. Researchers suggest that changes to the physical environment can complement other public safety strategies.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one advantage of improving street lighting is that it","choices":["can reduce certain crimes at relatively low cost.","replaces the need for police.","increases the number of vacant lots.","eliminates all crime."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Sometimes at lower cost than additional patrols.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4048e10a74","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-358","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some biologists have argued that animals that live in groups always benefit from doing so, because groups offer protection from predators.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Group living can reduce predation, but it also has costs. Animals in large groups must compete for food, and diseases spread more easily among closely packed individuals. Whether group living is beneficial overall depends on factors such as food availability and predation pressure.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging the protective benefit of groups while arguing that group living also has costs that vary with conditions","By arguing that animals should always live alone","By claiming that groups offer no protection from predators","By agreeing that group living is always beneficial"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Protection plus competition and disease. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5040a1480f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-358","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some biologists have argued that animals that live in groups always benefit from doing so, because groups offer protection from predators.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Group living can reduce predation, but it also has costs. Animals in large groups must compete for food, and diseases spread more easily among closely packed individuals. Whether group living is beneficial overall depends on factors such as food availability and predation pressure.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely object to the word \"always\" in Text 1 because","choices":["predators avoid groups.","group living never protects animals.","all animals live in groups.","the benefits of group living depend on circumstances."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benefit \"depends on factors such as food availability.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bc048bc8b7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-359","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiences prefer films based on original stories rather than sequels and remakes.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Box office data from recent decades show that sequels and remakes have frequently been among the highest-earning films each year. Surveys, however, suggest that many viewers say they want more original films, even as they buy tickets to familiar franchises. What audiences say and what they choose may differ.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that surveys accurately predict ticket sales","By claiming that audiences never watch sequels","By noting that ticket sales favor sequels and remakes even though many viewers say they want originality","By agreeing that original films always earn more than sequels"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Stated preferences differ from purchasing behavior. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8c1f93342e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-359","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiences prefer films based on original stories rather than sequels and remakes.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Box office data from recent decades show that sequels and remakes have frequently been among the highest-earning films each year. Surveys, however, suggest that many viewers say they want more original films, even as they buy tickets to familiar franchises. What audiences say and what they choose may differ.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the critics' claim in Text 1","choices":["reflects what many viewers say but not what box office data show.","ignores surveys of viewers.","applies only to animated films.","is fully supported by box office data."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Surveys support it; ticket sales do not. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0cdf75dca5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-360","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some ecologists claim that cats kept indoors live healthier lives than cats allowed outdoors.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Surveys of veterinarians and pet insurance records suggest that indoor cats face lower risks of injury from traffic, fights, and certain infectious diseases, and often live longer. However, some indoor cats become overweight or bored without enough activity, so veterinarians recommend providing play and climbing spaces.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, veterinarians recommend play and climbing spaces for indoor cats in order to","choices":["prevent weight gain and boredom.","help cats find food outdoors.","reduce traffic injuries.","cure infectious diseases."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Some indoor cats become \"overweight or bored.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.2df2788028","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-361","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians describe a famous explorer as the first person to reach a particular region of North America.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Indigenous peoples had lived in the region for thousands of years before the explorer arrived, and oral histories and archaeological sites document their long presence. The explorer's journey was significant for European knowledge of the area, but describing him as the first to reach it overlooks the people who already lived there.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that archaeological sites are unreliable","By claiming that the explorer never traveled to the region","By agreeing that the explorer was the first person in the region","By pointing out that Indigenous peoples had lived in the region long before the explorer arrived"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Indigenous presence predates the explorer. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ddb220d87e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-361","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians describe a famous explorer as the first person to reach a particular region of North America.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Indigenous peoples had lived in the region for thousands of years before the explorer arrived, and oral histories and archaeological sites document their long presence. The explorer's journey was significant for European knowledge of the area, but describing him as the first to reach it overlooks the people who already lived there.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, evidence of Indigenous presence in the region comes from","choices":["the explorer's diary alone.","modern maps only.","oral histories and archaeological sites.","European census records."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Oral histories and archaeological sites document their long presence.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3475f6c489","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-363","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that humans use only ten percent of their brains.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging shows activity throughout the brain, though not all regions are equally active at the same moment. Damage to almost any area of the brain can affect abilities, which would be unlikely if most of the brain went unused. Neuroscientists regard the ten-percent idea as a myth.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions brain damage mainly to","choices":["explain how imaging devices work.","suggest that most brain regions are unnecessary.","argue that people should avoid injuries.","show that nearly every area of the brain serves a function."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Damage anywhere affects abilities. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7185b4780c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-363","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that humans use only ten percent of their brains.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging shows activity throughout the brain, though not all regions are equally active at the same moment. Damage to almost any area of the brain can affect abilities, which would be unlikely if most of the brain went unused. Neuroscientists regard the ten-percent idea as a myth.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which statement is accurate?","choices":["Neuroscientists support the ten-percent idea.","Brain imaging cannot detect activity.","Only ten percent of the brain is ever active.","Not all brain regions are equally active at every moment."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Not all regions are equally active at the same moment.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bbeae0f6ff","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-364","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that tall residential towers are the best way to house growing city populations.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Towers can house many people on small plots of land, but mid-rise buildings of four to eight stories can achieve similar densities across a neighborhood while keeping residents closer to street life. Some studies suggest that residents of mid-rise neighborhoods report stronger connections with neighbors than residents of isolated high-rise towers.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that towers","choices":["cannot be built in cities.","can house many people on small plots of land.","strengthen connections among neighbors.","are always better than mid-rise buildings."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Towers can house many people on small plots.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ffc55d5629","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-365","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that grades motivate students to work hard.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Grades can encourage effort, especially for students focused on college admissions. But research suggests that when grades become the main goal, some students choose easier tasks to protect their scores and lose interest in learning for its own sake. Feedback that explains how to improve may sustain motivation more effectively for many students.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that grades never motivate anyone","By arguing that feedback reduces motivation","By agreeing that grades are the best motivation for all students","By acknowledging that grades can encourage effort while arguing that they can also undermine interest and challenge-seeking"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Grades help some but can have drawbacks. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.02474a08c1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-365","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that grades motivate students to work hard.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Grades can encourage effort, especially for students focused on college admissions. But research suggests that when grades become the main goal, some students choose easier tasks to protect their scores and lose interest in learning for its own sake. Feedback that explains how to improve may sustain motivation more effectively for many students.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 suggests that an alternative way to sustain motivation is","choices":["assigning more tests.","eliminating all assignments.","giving every student the same grade.","feedback that explains how to improve."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Explanatory feedback \"may sustain motivation more effectively.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.456f67aeeb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-366","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians claim that medieval people believed the Earth was flat.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Educated writers throughout the Middle Ages generally accepted that the Earth was round, a view inherited from ancient Greek scholars. Textbooks used in medieval universities described a spherical Earth. The idea that medieval people commonly believed in a flat Earth became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions university textbooks mainly to","choices":["argue that universities were expensive.","provide evidence of what educated medieval people believed.","describe how books were printed.","show that textbooks contained errors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Textbooks described a spherical Earth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c0c439a2cb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-367","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people argue that working longer hours always leads to greater productivity.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of factory and office workers have found that output per hour tends to decline when work weeks become very long. Beyond a certain point, fatigue leads to more errors and accidents, so additional hours add little or even reduce total output. Many companies have found that moderate schedules with adequate rest sustain productivity better.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that longer hours always increase productivity","By presenting evidence that very long hours can reduce productivity because of fatigue","By arguing that rest reduces productivity","By claiming that workers never make errors"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Output per hour falls, and errors rise. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3cfd7c8840","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-368","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video calls can fully replace in-person meetings for business teams.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Video calls save travel time and allow teams in different cities to meet easily. However, surveys of workers report that brainstorming and building trust with new colleagues often feel more difficult over video, and informal conversations before and after meetings are less common. Many teams now mix video calls with occasional in-person gatherings.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one thing that tends to happen less during video calls is","choices":["scheduled discussion.","saving travel time.","informal conversation before and after meetings.","meetings between people in different cities."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Informal conversations ... are less common.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4a1a40ae18","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-368","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video calls can fully replace in-person meetings for business teams.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Video calls save travel time and allow teams in different cities to meet easily. However, surveys of workers report that brainstorming and building trust with new colleagues often feel more difficult over video, and informal conversations before and after meetings are less common. Many teams now mix video calls with occasional in-person gatherings.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the critics in Text 1 that video calls","choices":["make it easier for teams in different cities to meet.","eliminate the need for travel entirely.","improve brainstorming.","are best for building trust with new colleagues."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They \"allow teams in different cities to meet easily.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f910bad842","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-369","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers argue that fresh vegetables are always more nutritious than frozen ones.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Vegetables are often frozen within hours of harvest, preserving many vitamins, while fresh vegetables may spend days in transport and storage, losing nutrients along the way. Studies comparing the two have found that frozen vegetables are often similar in nutrients to fresh ones and sometimes contain more of certain vitamins.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that fresh vegetables are always more nutritious","By claiming that frozen vegetables contain no vitamins","By presenting evidence that frozen vegetables can be as nutritious as fresh ones, and sometimes more so","By arguing that vegetables should not be eaten"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Quick freezing preserves nutrients. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5316b3ba90","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-369","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers argue that fresh vegetables are always more nutritious than frozen ones.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Vegetables are often frozen within hours of harvest, preserving many vitamins, while fresh vegetables may spend days in transport and storage, losing nutrients along the way. Studies comparing the two have found that frozen vegetables are often similar in nutrients to fresh ones and sometimes contain more of certain vitamins.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely object to the word \"always\" in Text 1 because","choices":["all vegetables are frozen before sale.","fresh vegetables are never nutritious.","nutrients cannot be measured.","frozen vegetables sometimes contain more of certain vitamins."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Frozen can sometimes be better. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.422f1ed62c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-370","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers say that the Industrial Revolution made life worse for ordinary workers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Early factory work was often dangerous, hours were long, and crowded cities bred disease. Over time, however, rising productivity contributed to higher wages, cheaper goods, and eventually to reforms such as limits on child labor and working hours. The period's effects on workers changed considerably over the course of the century.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one later development that helped workers was","choices":["reforms limiting child labor and working hours.","longer hours in factories.","more crowded cities.","higher prices for goods."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Reforms \"such as limits on child labor and working hours.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.14d900aeae","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-371","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have claimed that birds' feathers first evolved to help them fly.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fossils show that several dinosaurs that could not fly already had feathers. Some of these feathers were simple filaments that likely provided insulation, and others may have been used in displays to attract mates. Feathers may have been adapted for flight only later in the history of birds' ancestors.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that birds cannot fly","By claiming that dinosaurs never had feathers","By agreeing that feathers evolved only for flight","By presenting fossil evidence that feathers existed in non-flying dinosaurs and likely served other purposes first"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Flightless dinosaurs had feathers used for insulation or display. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4c9aca32e8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-373","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some energy experts argue that nuclear power is too dangerous to be part of efforts to reduce carbon emissions.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Nuclear accidents have caused serious harm, and long-term storage of radioactive waste remains a challenge. Yet measured by deaths per unit of electricity produced, nuclear power has a safety record comparable to wind and solar and far better than coal. Some researchers argue that its risks should be weighed against the harms of fossil fuels.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that nuclear waste poses no challenges","By acknowledging nuclear power's risks while arguing that its safety record compares favorably with fossil fuels","By arguing that coal is safer than nuclear power","By agreeing that nuclear power should be excluded from all energy plans"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Risks exist, but safety record is comparable to renewables. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9def6eb6b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-373","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some energy experts argue that nuclear power is too dangerous to be part of efforts to reduce carbon emissions.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Nuclear accidents have caused serious harm, and long-term storage of radioactive waste remains a challenge. Yet measured by deaths per unit of electricity produced, nuclear power has a safety record comparable to wind and solar and far better than coal. Some researchers argue that its risks should be weighed against the harms of fossil fuels.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, nuclear power's safety record, measured by deaths per unit of electricity, is","choices":["the worst of all energy sources.","impossible to measure.","comparable to wind and solar and better than coal.","worse than coal."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Comparable to wind and solar and far better than coal.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a701c044f8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-374","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers argue that handwriting is no longer an important skill in the digital age.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Adults increasingly type rather than write by hand, but studies of young children suggest that learning to form letters by hand helps them recognize letters and learn to read. Handwritten notes may also help some students remember information better than typed notes. The value of handwriting may lie partly in its effects on learning, not only in its everyday use.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the writers in Text 1 that","choices":["handwriting is essential for all adult jobs.","typing harms children's reading.","notes should never be typed.","adults increasingly type instead of writing by hand."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Adults increasingly type rather than write by hand.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.69864afe70","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-375","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some sports commentators claim that home teams win more often mainly because of cheering crowds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When sports leagues played games without fans during a period of restrictions, the home advantage shrank in some sports but did not disappear. Researchers suggest that familiarity with the venue, reduced travel fatigue, and referees' decisions all contribute, with crowds being one factor among several.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, when games were played without fans, the home advantage","choices":["vanished completely.","could not be measured.","shrank in some sports but did not disappear.","grew much larger."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It \"shrank in some sports but did not disappear.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.24990862a8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-376","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some commentators argue that social media has made teenagers less happy.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large surveys find a small average association between heavy social media use and lower well-being among teenagers, but the effect varies widely. For some teens, especially those who use it passively for many hours, social media is linked to worse mood; others use it to maintain friendships and find support. Researchers caution that the average link is modest and does not show that social media causes unhappiness.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which teens are more likely to show a link between social media and worse mood?","choices":["Those who never use it","Those who use it to maintain friendships","Those who find support online","Those who use it passively for many hours"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Passive heavy use is linked to worse mood. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8bb25452ca","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-378","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art historians argue that a famous painter worked alone and developed his distinctive style entirely by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Workshop records show that the painter employed several assistants who prepared canvases, mixed pigments, and sometimes painted backgrounds. Letters also reveal that he studied and admired the works of earlier artists. His style was distinctive, but it developed within a network of collaborators and influences.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that the painter worked entirely alone","By claiming that the painter's style was not distinctive","By arguing that the painter's work involved assistants and drew on earlier artists' influence","By arguing that assistants painted all of his works"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Records show assistants and influences. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.94841aba5d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-379","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some psychologists argue that people's personalities remain fixed after early adulthood.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Long-term studies that followed people for decades found that many personality traits change gradually across adulthood. On average, people become more conscientious and emotionally stable as they age, though individuals differ in how much they change. Personality appears more stable in adulthood than in childhood but not entirely fixed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that personality changes rapidly every year","By arguing that personality cannot be measured","By agreeing that personality never changes after early adulthood","By presenting evidence that personality continues to change gradually during adulthood"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Traits shift across decades. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0240f1ec97","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-379","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some psychologists argue that people's personalities remain fixed after early adulthood.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Long-term studies that followed people for decades found that many personality traits change gradually across adulthood. On average, people become more conscientious and emotionally stable as they age, though individuals differ in how much they change. Personality appears more stable in adulthood than in childhood but not entirely fixed.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, on average people tend to become more","choices":["conscientious and emotionally stable as they age.","anxious as they age.","impulsive with age.","similar to their childhood selves."],"answer":"A","explanation":"More conscientious and emotionally stable. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f281a33001","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-379","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some psychologists argue that people's personalities remain fixed after early adulthood.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Long-term studies that followed people for decades found that many personality traits change gradually across adulthood. On average, people become more conscientious and emotionally stable as they age, though individuals differ in how much they change. Personality appears more stable in adulthood than in childhood but not entirely fixed.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that personality","choices":["is completely fixed after twenty.","is more stable in adulthood than in childhood.","has nothing to do with age.","changes more in adulthood than childhood."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"More stable in adulthood than in childhood.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.60c7bbe8a0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-380","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some travelers believe that visiting popular tourist destinations during peak season offers the best experience.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Peak season often brings pleasant weather and full schedules of events. Yet crowds, long lines, and higher prices can make visits stressful. Many travelers who visit during the \"shoulder seasons\" just before or after peak times report mild weather, lower costs, and a more relaxed experience.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that peak season has no advantages","By agreeing that peak season is always best","By acknowledging peak season's advantages while suggesting that shoulder seasons can offer a better overall experience for many travelers","By arguing that travelers should avoid tourist destinations"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Shoulder seasons offer mild weather and lower costs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.85d54e18dd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-381","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists claim that honeybees are the most important pollinators for wild plants.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees pollinate many crops, but studies of wild plant communities have found that native bees, such as bumblebees and solitary bees, often do more of the pollination. In some habitats, large honeybee colonies may even compete with native bees for flowers.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a possible concern about large honeybee colonies is that they","choices":["pollinate too few crops.","increase native bee populations.","may compete with native bees for flowers.","cannot find flowers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They \"may even compete with native bees.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.cced6b7a19","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-382","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that a certain ancient empire fell primarily because of economic problems.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The empire did suffer from inflation and heavy taxation, but it also faced repeated invasions, civil wars, and outbreaks of disease. Historians increasingly describe its decline as the result of many interacting pressures rather than a single cause.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging economic problems while arguing that many other pressures also contributed to the empire's decline","By agreeing that economics was the only cause","By arguing that the empire never declined","By claiming that the empire had no economic problems"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Multiple interacting pressures. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d5ad9632aa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-382","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that a certain ancient empire fell primarily because of economic problems.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The empire did suffer from inflation and heavy taxation, but it also faced repeated invasions, civil wars, and outbreaks of disease. Historians increasingly describe its decline as the result of many interacting pressures rather than a single cause.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which factor contributed to the empire's decline in addition to economic problems?","choices":["Declining taxes","Rapid population growth","Repeated invasions","A lack of civil wars"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Repeated invasions, civil wars, and outbreaks of disease.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5d657256fd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-383","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers argue that owning a pet improves people's health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some studies have linked dog ownership with more physical activity, since dog owners take regular walks. Other research finds that pet owners differ from non-owners in income, age, and lifestyle, which could explain some health differences. Pets may offer benefits, but the evidence that pets themselves improve health is mixed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that dog owners never walk","By noting that some benefits have been linked to pets but that differences between owners and non-owners make the evidence mixed","By claiming that pets harm health","By agreeing that pets always improve health"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Benefits are possible, but confounders exist. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.37da252249","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-383","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers argue that owning a pet improves people's health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some studies have linked dog ownership with more physical activity, since dog owners take regular walks. Other research finds that pet owners differ from non-owners in income, age, and lifestyle, which could explain some health differences. Pets may offer benefits, but the evidence that pets themselves improve health is mixed.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, dog ownership has been linked to more physical activity because","choices":["dog owners take regular walks.","dogs discourage walking.","owners are younger than non-owners.","dogs require no exercise."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Dog owners take regular walks.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6bf6c68691","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-384","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising the minimum wage always leads to large job losses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing neighboring regions with different minimum wage increases have often found small or no effects on employment for moderate increases. Researchers caution, however, that very large increases, or increases in areas with low average wages, might have different effects that are harder to predict.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that minimum wages should be eliminated","By claiming that minimum wage increases always create jobs","By presenting evidence that moderate increases often have small employment effects, while noting uncertainty about very large increases","By agreeing that any increase causes large job losses"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Small effects for moderate increases; uncertainty for large ones. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6bb480aeb5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-384","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising the minimum wage always leads to large job losses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing neighboring regions with different minimum wage increases have often found small or no effects on employment for moderate increases. Researchers caution, however, that very large increases, or increases in areas with low average wages, might have different effects that are harder to predict.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, researchers are less certain about the effects of","choices":["very large minimum wage increases.","moderate increases in neighboring regions.","wages above the minimum.","increases that have already been studied."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Very large increases \"might have different effects.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9cadbdc2b2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-385","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should not use calculators until they have mastered arithmetic by hand.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research suggests that calculators, used thoughtfully, do not harm basic skills and can let students focus on problem solving and patterns. However, studies also show that students benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts and developing number sense before relying heavily on calculators.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, calculators used thoughtfully can allow students to","choices":["skip learning numbers.","avoid all math classes.","memorize answers without thinking.","focus on problem solving and patterns."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They let students \"focus on problem solving and patterns.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3704b96651","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-386","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that whales are too large to be affected by small plastic particles in the ocean.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Filter-feeding whales swallow enormous volumes of water to catch tiny prey, and researchers analyzing their feeding have estimated that some whales may ingest millions of microplastic particles daily. The long-term health effects are still being studied, but the scale of exposure suggests that size does not protect whales from plastic pollution.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that microplastics do not exist in oceans","By agreeing that whales are unaffected by plastic","By claiming that whales avoid eating prey","By arguing that whales' feeding methods may expose them to large amounts of microplastics despite their size"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Filter feeding leads to heavy exposure. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c582f31051","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-386","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that whales are too large to be affected by small plastic particles in the ocean.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Filter-feeding whales swallow enormous volumes of water to catch tiny prey, and researchers analyzing their feeding have estimated that some whales may ingest millions of microplastic particles daily. The long-term health effects are still being studied, but the scale of exposure suggests that size does not protect whales from plastic pollution.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, filter-feeding whales may ingest many microplastics because they","choices":["live only near beaches.","swallow huge volumes of water while feeding.","eat plastic deliberately.","have very small mouths."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They \"swallow enormous volumes of water.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.84118e1077","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-387","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people who multitask are more productive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments show that switching between tasks takes time and increases errors, because the brain must reorient each time attention shifts. People who frequently multitask often perform worse on tests of attention than people who multitask less, although they tend to believe they are skilled at it.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, frequent multitaskers tend to","choices":["believe they are good at multitasking despite performing worse on attention tests.","avoid switching tasks.","perform better on attention tests.","make fewer errors than others."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They \"tend to believe they are skilled at it.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.27548eae7d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-387","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people who multitask are more productive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments show that switching between tasks takes time and increases errors, because the brain must reorient each time attention shifts. People who frequently multitask often perform worse on tests of attention than people who multitask less, although they tend to believe they are skilled at it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 challenges the claim made in Text 1.","Text 2 agrees but adds that multitasking is fun.","Text 2 discusses computer processors.","Text 2 supports the claim made in Text 1."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Evidence counters productivity claims. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c49b72d002","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-388","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban officials argue that banning cars from city centers will harm local businesses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When several European cities closed central districts to most car traffic, some shops initially reported fewer customers. Within a few years, however, foot traffic increased in many of these districts, and surveys found that pedestrians often spent more time and money in shops than drivers had. Results varied by city and depended on transit access.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that shops had more customers immediately after every ban","By noting that early losses were often followed by increased foot traffic and spending, though results varied","By agreeing that car bans always harm businesses permanently","By arguing that transit access is irrelevant"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Initial dips, later gains, with variation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.56a58efafa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-390","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that fish do not feel pain because they lack certain brain structures found in mammals.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fish have nerve receptors that respond to harmful stimuli, and experiments show that fish exposed to such stimuli change their behavior, avoiding areas where they were harmed and responding to pain-relieving drugs. Although fish brains differ from mammal brains, some scientists argue that these findings suggest fish may experience something like pain.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that fish brains are identical to mammal brains","By arguing that pain-relieving drugs have no effect on fish","By presenting behavioral and physiological evidence that fish may experience something like pain despite brain differences","By agreeing that fish cannot feel pain"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Receptors, avoidance, and response to drugs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.79b0b8d4bf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-390","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that fish do not feel pain because they lack certain brain structures found in mammals.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fish have nerve receptors that respond to harmful stimuli, and experiments show that fish exposed to such stimuli change their behavior, avoiding areas where they were harmed and responding to pain-relieving drugs. Although fish brains differ from mammal brains, some scientists argue that these findings suggest fish may experience something like pain.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the researchers in Text 1 that","choices":["the question has been completely settled.","fish cannot sense harmful stimuli.","fish brains differ from mammal brains.","fish have no nerves."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Although fish brains differ from mammal brains.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.612110fe8d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-391","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children should be praised for being smart to build their confidence.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies have found that children praised for intelligence were more likely to avoid challenging tasks afterward, perhaps fearing they would look less smart if they failed. Children praised for effort were more willing to try harder problems and persisted longer after mistakes.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that effort is unimportant","By presenting evidence that praising effort encourages persistence more than praising intelligence","By claiming that children should never be praised","By agreeing that praising intelligence always builds confidence"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Effort praise leads to more challenge-seeking. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ccf73482cf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-391","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children should be praised for being smart to build their confidence.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies have found that children praised for intelligence were more likely to avoid challenging tasks afterward, perhaps fearing they would look less smart if they failed. Children praised for effort were more willing to try harder problems and persisted longer after mistakes.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 restates Text 1.","Text 2 presents evidence that complicates the approach recommended in Text 1.","Text 2 supports the approach in Text 1.","Text 2 discusses adult workers."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Intelligence praise may backfire. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c64212487e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-392","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some geologists argue that earthquakes can be predicted precisely by monitoring animal behavior.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>There are many anecdotes of animals behaving strangely before earthquakes, but systematic studies have not found reliable patterns. Animals behave unusually for many reasons, and reports tend to be remembered after an earthquake occurs. Scientists instead focus on estimating the long-term probability of earthquakes in particular regions.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions that reports \"tend to be remembered after an earthquake occurs\" mainly to","choices":["show that animals remember earthquakes.","argue that scientists ignore animals.","explain how earthquakes form.","suggest why anecdotes may seem more convincing than they are."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Hindsight makes anecdotes seem predictive. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ced9fb32fd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-393","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that suburbs are always worse for the environment than cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>City residents generally drive less and live in smaller homes, which tends to reduce energy use per person. However, some suburbs with good transit and compact development have lower emissions than car-dependent areas within city limits. The environmental impact depends more on density, transit, and housing type than on whether an area is labeled urban or suburban.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that cities always produce more emissions","By arguing that transit has no effect on emissions","By agreeing that every suburb is worse than every city neighborhood","By arguing that environmental impact depends on factors such as density and transit rather than simply on whether an area is a suburb"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Design matters more than labels. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c7742f623c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-393","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that suburbs are always worse for the environment than cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>City residents generally drive less and live in smaller homes, which tends to reduce energy use per person. However, some suburbs with good transit and compact development have lower emissions than car-dependent areas within city limits. The environmental impact depends more on density, transit, and housing type than on whether an area is labeled urban or suburban.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the planners in Text 1 that city residents","choices":["always live in larger homes.","produce more emissions than all suburbanites.","never use transit.","tend to drive less."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"City residents generally drive less.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5d15de6657","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-394","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that the ancient city of Troy described in epic poetry was purely imaginary.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at a site in western Turkey have uncovered a city with multiple layers of settlement, including one destroyed around the time associated with the legendary war. While the poems contain myth and exaggeration, many archaeologists believe they may preserve memories of a real city and conflicts in the region.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that the poems","choices":["are exact historical accounts.","contain myth and exaggeration.","were written by archaeologists.","describe a city in Egypt."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"The poems contain myth and exaggeration.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a1ab02ec42","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-394","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that the ancient city of Troy described in epic poetry was purely imaginary.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at a site in western Turkey have uncovered a city with multiple layers of settlement, including one destroyed around the time associated with the legendary war. While the poems contain myth and exaggeration, many archaeologists believe they may preserve memories of a real city and conflicts in the region.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the excavated city is significant because one of its layers","choices":["contains a copy of the poems.","was destroyed around the time associated with the legendary war.","was built in the modern era.","proves every event in the poems."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The destruction layer matches the war's timing. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.01aded767c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-395","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some health writers argue that vitamin supplements are necessary for everyone.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>People with certain conditions, diets, or life stages may benefit from specific supplements; for example, pregnant women are often advised to take folic acid. For most healthy adults who eat a varied diet, however, large studies have found little benefit from routine multivitamins in preventing major diseases.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions folic acid for pregnant women mainly to","choices":["criticize doctors' advice.","give an example of a group that may benefit from a specific supplement.","argue that all supplements are unnecessary.","explain how vitamins are made."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An example of targeted benefit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.17dee3caf9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-395","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some health writers argue that vitamin supplements are necessary for everyone.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>People with certain conditions, diets, or life stages may benefit from specific supplements; for example, pregnant women are often advised to take folic acid. For most healthy adults who eat a varied diet, however, large studies have found little benefit from routine multivitamins in preventing major diseases.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, large studies found that routine multivitamins for healthy adults with varied diets","choices":["caused serious harm.","were required by law.","showed little benefit in preventing major diseases.","prevented most diseases."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Little benefit from routine multivitamins.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."}
